// Seeding at boot (aether DB6): after `conn.migrate(dir)` brings the schema up to date, `conn.seed(dir)`
// runs every re-runnable seed file under a directory — dev data, NOT tracked history, so it runs every
// time. Idempotent inserts (`... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, which both backends accept) make a re-run a
// no-op. Run from the repo root so the relative paths resolve:
//
//   noeta run examples/para-db-demo/seed_demo.noe
//
// This is the SELF-SEEDING path — a program seeding itself, with no CLI in the picture — so the
// directory it reads is SQL-only (`boot_seeds/`). A `.noe` seed is a Noeta program, and only the CLI
// can load, check and run one: `conn.seed(dir)` says so plainly if it meets one, rather than skipping
// it. The `noeta migrate` command drives `seeds/`, which has both body languages side by side.
use para.db

conn = db.connect("sqlite::memory:")

// 1. Migrate: create the schema (and its tracked seed rows Ada/Bob from migration 0002).
applied = conn.migrate("examples/para-db-demo/migrations")
echo "applied ${applied} migration(s)"

// 2. Seed: add re-runnable dev users. Returns how many seed files ran (never a no-op skip — seeds are
//    not tracked, so every file runs every call).
seeded = conn.seed("examples/para-db-demo/boot_seeds")
echo "ran ${seeded} seed file(s)"

// 3. Seeding again runs the same files again — but the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING idiom keeps the data
//    stable, on SQLite and on PostgreSQL alike.
conn.seed("examples/para-db-demo/boot_seeds")

rows = conn.query("SELECT id, name FROM users ORDER BY id", [])
echo "users -> ${rows.len()}"
for row in rows {
    id = row["id"]
    name = row["name"]
    echo "  #${id} ${name}"
}

conn.close()
